  "result": {
    "INDEX-1<SEP>How would a basic project in PyGame look like? I'd like the example to include keyboard event handling so that pressing ESC will quit the game and also print the FPS counter in the left top corner of the window.<SEP>Here is a simple example of a Pygame game that displays FPS counter in the top left corner and quits the game when the 'ESC' key is pressed:\n\nimport pygame\nimport time\nimport sys\n\npygame.init()\n\n# Set up the display\nscreen = pygame.display.set_mode((640, 480))\npygame.display.set_caption(\"Simple Pygame Game\")\n\n# Set up the clock\nclock = pygame.time.Clock()\n\n# Set up the font\nfont = pygame.font.Font(None, 36)\n\n# Set up the FPS counter\nfps = 0\nfps_text = font.render(\"FPS: 0\", True, (0, 255, 0))\n\n# Game loop\nrunning = True\nwhile running:\n    # Event handling\n    for event in pygame.event.get():\n        if event.type == pygame.QUIT:\n            running = False\n        if event.type == pygame.KEYDOWN:\n            if event.key == pygame.K_ESCAPE:\n                running = False\n\n    # Clear the screen\n    screen.fill((0, 0, 0))\n\n    # Draw the FPS counter\n    fps_text = font.render(\"FPS: \" + str(int(clock.get_fps())), True, (0, 255, 0))\n    screen.blit(fps_text, (10, 10))\n\n    # Update the display\n    pygame.display.flip()\n\n    # Cap the framerate\n    clock.tick(60)\n\n# Quit Pygame\npygame.quit()\nsys.exit()": 3.375,
    "INDEX-1<SEP>How would a basic project in PyGame look like? I'd like the example to include keyboard event handling so that pressing ESC will quit the game and also print the FPS counter in the left top corner of the window.<SEP>Here is an example of a basic PyGame project with keyboard event handling and an FPS counter in the top left corner of the window:\n\nimport pygame\nimport time\nimport random\n\npygame.init()\n\n# Set up the display\nwidth = 800\nheight = 600\nwindow = pygame.display.set_mode((width, height))\npygame.display.set_caption(\"Basic PyGame Project\")\n\n# Set up the clock\nclock = pygame.time.Clock()\n\n# Set up the font\nfont = pygame.font.SysFont(\"Arial\", 24)\n\n# Main game loop\nrunning = True\nwhile running:\n    # Event handling\n    for event in pygame.event.get():\n        if event.type == pygame.QUIT:\n            running = False\n        if event.type == pygame.KEYDOWN:\n            if event.key == pygame.K_ESCAPE:\n                running = False\n\n    # Draw the FPS counter\n    fps_text = font.render(f\"FPS: {int(clock.get_fps())}\", True, (255, 255, 255))\n    window.blit(fps_text, (10, 10))\n\n    # Update the display\n    pygame.display.update()\n\n    # Maintain the desired FPS\n    clock.tick(60)\n\n# Quit the game\npygame.quit()": 2.65625,
    "INDEX-1<SEP>How would a basic project in PyGame look like? I'd like the example to include keyboard event handling so that pressing ESC will quit the game and also print the FPS counter in the left top corner of the window.<SEP>Here is a simple project in PyGame that includes keyboard event handling and an FPS counter in the top left corner of the window:\n\n```python\nimport pygame\nimport sys\nimport time\nimport random\n\npygame.init()\n\n# Set up the display\nwidth, height = 800, 600\ndisplay = pygame.display.set_mode((width, height))\npygame.display.set_caption(\"Simple PyGame Project\")\n\n# Set up the clock\nclock = pygame.time.Clock()\n\n# Set up the FPS counter\nfont = pygame.font.Font(None, 36)\nfps_text = font.render(\"FPS: 0\", True, (0, 0, 0))\n\n# Main game loop\nrunning = True\nwhile running:\n    # Event handling\n    for event in pygame.event.get():\n        if event.type == pygame.QUIT:\n            running = False\n        if event.type == pygame.KEYDOWN:\n            if event.key == pygame.K_ESCAPE:\n                running = False\n\n    # Update the FPS counter\n    fps = clock.get_fps()\n    fps_text = font.render(f\"FPS: {int(fps)}\", True, (0, 0, 0))\n\n    # Fill the display\n    display.fill((255, 255, 255))\n\n    # Draw the FPS counter\n    display.blit(fps_text, (10, 10))\n\n    # Update the display\n    pygame.display.flip()\n\n    # Cap the frame rate\n    clock.tick(60)\n\n# Quit the game\npygame.quit()\nsys.exit()\n```\n\nThis example creates a window with a white background and a black FPS counter in the top left corner. The game loop checks for keyboard events and updates the FPS counter every frame. The game will quit when the user presses the ESC key.": 5.25,
    "INDEX-1<SEP>How would a basic project in PyGame look like? I'd like the example to include keyboard event handling so that pressing ESC will quit the game and also print the FPS counter in the left top corner of the window.<SEP>Here's a basic Pygame project that includes keyboard event handling to quit the game when the ESC key is pressed and an FPS counter in the top left corner of the window. It's important to note that the FPS counter will only update once per frame, and the actual FPS will depend on the speed of the computer running the game.\n\n```python\nimport pygame\nimport time\nimport sys\n\n# Initialize the game engine\npygame.init()\n\n# Define constants\nWIDTH = 800\nHEIGHT = 600\nFPS = 30\nBG_COLOR = (0, 0, 0)\nTEXT_COLOR = (255, 255, 255)\n\n# Create the game window\nscreen = pygame.display.set_mode((WIDTH, HEIGHT))\npygame.display.set_caption(\"Basic Pygame Project\")\n\n# Initialize the clock\nclock = pygame.time.Clock()\n\n# Create a font for the FPS counter\nfont = pygame.font.Font(None, 36)\n\n# Main game loop\nrunning = True\nwhile running:\n    # Handle events\n    for event in pygame.event.get():\n        if event.type == pygame.QUIT:\n            running = False\n        if event.type == pygame.KEYDOWN:\n            if event.key == pygame.K_ESCAPE:\n                running = False\n\n    # Draw the background\n    screen.fill(BG_COLOR)\n\n    # Draw the FPS counter\n    fps_text = font.render(f\"FPS: {int(clock.get_fps())}\", True, TEXT_COLOR)\n    screen.blit(fps_text, (10, 10))\n\n    # Update the display\n    pygame.display.flip()\n\n    # Limit the frame rate\n    clock.tick(FPS)\n\n# Quit the game engine\npygame.quit()\nsys.exit()\n```": 5.0,
    "INDEX-1<SEP>How would a basic project in PyGame look like? I'd like the example to include keyboard event handling so that pressing ESC will quit the game and also print the FPS counter in the left top corner of the window.<SEP>Here is a basic example of a PyGame project, including keyboard event handling and an FPS counter.\n\nimport pygame\nimport time\nimport sys\n\npygame.init()\n\n# Set up the display\nWIDTH, HEIGHT = 800, 600\ndisplay = pygame.display.set_mode((WIDTH, HEIGHT))\npygame.display.set_caption(\"PyGame Project\")\n\n# Set up the clock\nclock = pygame.time.Clock()\n\n# Set up the FPS counter\nfont = pygame.font.Font(None, 36)\nfps_text = font.render(\"FPS: 0\", True, (255, 255, 255))\n\n# Game loop\nrunning = True\nwhile running:\n    # Event handling\n    for event in pygame.event.get():\n        if event.type == pygame.QUIT:\n            running = False\n        if event.type == pygame.KEYDOWN:\n            if event.key == pygame.K_ESCAPE:\n                running = False\n\n    # Update the FPS counter\n    now = pygame.time.get_ticks()\n    dt = now - prev_time\n    fps = int(1000 / dt)\n    fps_text = font.render(\"FPS: %d\" % fps, True, (255, 255, 255))\n    prev_time = now\n\n    # Fill the display\n    display.fill((0, 0, 0))\n\n    # Draw the FPS counter\n    display.blit(fps_text, (0, 0))\n\n    # Update the display\n    pygame.display.flip()\n\n    # Cap the FPS\n    clock.tick(60)\n\n# Quit PyGame\npygame.quit()\nsys.exit()": 1.5625,
